                <pb facs="0302" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/10451: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-05-21T17:00:00+08:00">May 21, 1929—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 22—8:30 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>408. My 394, May 17, 6 p.m.<anchor n="41" xml:id="d67-f1"/> Following from
                    American Consul at Tsingtao: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“May 21, 11 a.m. Japanese evacuation of Shantung completed according to
                            schedule when last troops and General Yasumitsu sailed from Tsingtao
                            yesterday. Five hundred marines from the Chinese warship <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Chenhai</hi> landed morning of 20th to cooperate
                            with Government officers of peace and order already here. City tranquil,
                            and no symptom of disorders apparent.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
